Short answer: that error usually means either the browser blocked/failed to access the microphone (getUserMedia) or the recorded audio upload failed (network/CORS/server). I can be more exact if you share the click handler + recording/upload code and the console/network error. 

Quick checklist of the most common causes and how to check/fix them
- Microphone permission denied
  - Check browser permission prompt and Settings → Site Permissions → Microphone.
  - In code, catch getUserMedia errors and log error.name (e.g. "NotAllowedError", "NotFoundError").
- Not served over HTTPS (or not localhost)
  - navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia requires secure context (HTTPS or localhost).
- Browser doesn’t support the API
  - Verify navigator.mediaDevices and MediaRecorder exist in the console.
- MediaRecorder/stream lifecycle bugs
  - Make sure you start the recorder only after getUserMedia resolved and stop it correctly before sending.
- Network/upload problems
  - If your app uploads the audio to a server, check the Network tab for the request, its response code, CORS errors, or server-side failures. The ":network" suffix in your message suggests a failed upload.
- CORS / server rejects the request
  - Ensure the server returns appropriate Access-Control-Allow-Origin header for cross-origin requests.
- Large file/timeouts
  - Long recordings may exceed server limits; check server logs.

Minimal working example (record + upload) with error logging you can compare against your code:
```javascript
async function startRecordingAndUpload() {
  try {
    const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({ audio: true });
    const recorder = new MediaRecorder(stream);
    const chunks = [];

    recorder.ondataavailable = e => chunks.push(e.data);
    recorder.onerror = e => console.error('MediaRecorder error', e);

    recorder.start();
    console.log('Recording started');

    // stop after 3 seconds for demo
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 3000));
    recorder.stop();

    await new Promise(resolve => recorder.onstop = resolve);
    console.log('Recording stopped');

    const blob = new Blob(chunks, { type: 'audio/webm' });
    // show size and type for debugging
    console.log('Recorded blob', blob.size, blob.type);

    // Upload (example)
    const form = new FormData();
    form.append('file', blob, 'recording.webm');

    const res = await fetch('https://your-server.example/upload', {
      method: 'POST',
      body: form,
      // include credentials if needed
      // credentials: 'include'
    });

    if (!res.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Upload failed: ${res.status} ${res.statusText}`);
    }

    console.log('Upload successful', await res.json());
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Recording/upload error', err.name || '', err.message || err);
    // show more info for debugging
  }
}
```

Debugging steps to run now
1. Open DevTools → Console and Network.
2. Click the "click to speak" button, then copy the exact console error (including stack) and any failing network request details (response/status/CORS message).
3. Confirm: which browser and OS, and whether the site is served on HTTPS or localhost.

One focused question so I can help precisely:
- Please paste the exact click-to-speak handler and recording/upload code (or point me to the file), plus the exact console error or failing network request details.

