Thanks — I found the voice logic in js/app.js. That error message ("Could not record voice: network" or similar) is coming from the SpeechRecognition onerror handler:

recognition.onerror = function(event) {
  showToast("Voice Error", "Could not record voice: " + event.error);
  if (isRecording) {
    recognition.stop();
  }
};

What "network" means and common causes
- "network" is an error code from the Web Speech API (SpeechRecognition). It means the browser's speech recognition service could not be reached (remote ASR failed).
- Typical reasons:
  - No internet or intermittent connectivity — Chrome's recognition relies on an online service.
  - A firewall/proxy blocking the speech recognition endpoint (corporate networks can cause this).
  - Browser's speech service unavailable (server outage or restricted build).
  - Language/locale unsupported by the service.
  - You're testing from a non-secure context (file:// or http) — some features require HTTPS or localhost.
  - Microphone permission or device issues can produce other error codes (not-allowed, audio-capture, no-speech).
  - Browser does not fully support the Web Speech API (or it's disabled).

How to quickly confirm the root cause
1. Open DevTools → Console and watch any errors when you click "Click to Speak". The recognition.onerror event and any console.logs will help.
2. Check the toast message you got; the appended error string is the exact Web Speech error name (e.g. "network", "not-allowed", "audio-capture").
3. Verify:
   - Are you online? Try loading another page.
   - Is the site served over HTTPS or localhost?
   - Browser and version (Chrome on desktop is recommended). Some mobile browsers/embedded webviews lack support.
   - Microphone permission: click the lock icon in the address bar → Site permissions → Microphone allowed?
   - OS-level microphone permission (macOS/Windows privacy settings).
4. Try switching site/PC/network (home network vs company Wi‑Fi) to rule out firewall.

Suggested improvements / quick fixes in code
- Log the full error, give user-friendly guidance, and optionally fall back to text input when network errors occur. Replace the current onerror with this improved handler:

```javascript
recognition.onerror = function(event) {
  console.error("SpeechRecognition onerror:", event);
  var err = event && event.error ? event.error : "unknown";
  var friendly;

  switch (err) {
    case "network":
      friendly = "Network error: speech service unreachable. Check your Internet connection or network firewall.";
      break;
    case "not-allowed":
    case "permission-denied":
      friendly = "Microphone permission denied. Allow microphone access in the browser site settings.";
      break;
    case "service-not-allowed":
      friendly = "Speech service not allowed by this browser or environment.";
      break;
    case "no-speech":
      friendly = "No speech detected. Try speaking louder or check your microphone.";
      break;
    case "audio-capture":
      friendly = "Microphone not available or already in use by another app.";
      break;
    default:
      friendly = "Voice recording failed (" + err + "). Try again or use the text input.";
  }

  showToast("Voice Error", friendly);
  // Stop recognition cleanly if still running
  if (isRecording && recognition && typeof recognition.stop === "function") {
    try { recognition.stop(); } catch (e) { console.warn("Error stopping recognition", e); }
  }

  // Optional: switch user to text input automatically on network/permission issues
  if (err === "network" || err === "not-allowed" || err === "service-not-allowed") {
    switchInputType("text");
  }
};
```

Longer-term options if you need robust offline/enterprise behavior
- Use a client-side STT engine (Vosk via WebAssembly) for offline recognition.
- Or upload the recorded audio to your own speech-to-text backend (requires implementing getUserMedia + MediaRecorder and an upload endpoint).
- If you require better availability, use a paid speech API (Google Cloud, Azure, AWS) through your server.

If you want, I can:
- Add the error handler patch into your project (I can create an edit preview).
- Add console logging & diagnostic UI to show the raw event.error and event.message.
- Help implement a getUserMedia + MediaRecorder fallback for offline capture.

One focused question: which browser and OS are you testing on, and are you serving the site over HTTPS or file:// (or localhost)? Also paste the exact toast text or console output you see (e.g., "Could not record voice: network") and I’ll suggest the minimal change to fix it.

